name":"Graphs::Rigging::swinging_coil","metadata":{"compoundIsGraph":"true","author":"Autodesk","category":"Rigging","description":"An example graph demonstrating how to use the simulate_rods solver to simulate a transform chain with the shape of a coil realistically.","_notes":[{"type":"sticky_note","text":"By constraining the first two transforms, the first segment of the rod stays completely fixed in place. It can’t move or rotate, so the base of the chain is effectively locked. The next segments will still try to maintain their original shape and alignment relative to their neighbors.\n\nIf you only constrain the first transform, the base position is fixed, but its orientation is free to change. The first segment can still rotate around that point, so the base is fixed in place but not locked in rotation.\n\nTry to disconnect the second value node to see the difference."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Apply a procedural animation on the coil by rotating all transforms from the origin. You can tweak the rotation parameters on the `rotation_from_origin` node."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Create the pivot matrices for the skeleton. Start by generating the coil's positions procedurally. Then, generate oriented transform matrices from them."},{"type":"sticky_note","text":"Apply coloring on the joints. We initialize the joint colors using `default_color` and overwrite the colors of the constrained transforms with `constrained_color`. Finally, display the skeleton in the viewport."}],"_backdrops":[{"type":"backdrop","title":"Create a skeleton structure for a single joint chain and create the joint rest transforms"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Simulate transforms as a soft rod"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Create curly transform chain"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Define joint count"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Create parent indices"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Apply procedural animation"},{"type":"backdrop","title":"Define constrained transform indices"},{"type":"